The range form is the idiomatic iteration, and its two returns carry the subtlety:

```go
s := "héllo"
for i, r := range s {
    fmt.Println(i, r)
    // 0 104 — 'h' at byte 0
    // 1 233 — 'é' at byte 1 (2 bytes wide)
    // 3 108 — 'l' at byte 3 — offset JUMPED past é
    // 4 108, 5 111
}
```

Executed. The index is a BYTE OFFSET — advancing by each rune's UTF-8 width. The value is the decoded code point — safe for character logic.

The byte-level alternative:

```go
for i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {
    b := s[i]      // 104, 195, 169, 108, 108, 111
}
```

No offset jumps — but values at multi-byte positions are encoding fragments, not characters.

The mistake being tested: combining the classic loop with character comparisons — s[i] == 'é' never fires (s[i] is byte 195, not rune 233). That class of bug is why range is the default.

The rune-safe reversal:

```go
func reverse(s string) string {
    r := []rune(s)
    for i, j := 0, len(r)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
        r[i], r[j] = r[j], r[i]
    }
    return string(r)
}

reverse("hello")  // "olleh"
reverse("héllo") // "olléh" — é moves as ONE unit
```

The byte version and why it's wrong for Unicode:

```go
b := []byte(s)
for i, j := 0, len(b)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
    b[i], b[j] = b[j], b[i]
}
// "héllo" → corrupted — é's two bytes (195 169) become (169 195)
// — an invalid rune that decodes to U+FFFD garbage
```

The mechanics of the corruption: é is stored as two bytes; byte-reversal swaps the halves independently — they end up adjacent but in the wrong order, which is no longer é's encoding. The output string holds invalid UTF-8.

The costs of the safe version: []rune decode (O(n) + allocation), the swap (O(n/2)), the re-encode (O(n) + allocation). Three passes — the honest price of character-level correctness. The byte version is two passes and allocation-free — and only correct on KNOWN-ASCII input, a guarantee real text doesn't make.

The edge worth one sentence: rune reversal reverses code points, not graphemes — "é" as e + combining accent stays two runes and the combining accent ends up attached to the wrong character. True grapheme-safe reversal needs a segmenter (x/text or uniseg) — the rune version is the standard, correct-for-most answer.
